														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3823977">
									

<p>It is certainly well written, that's for sure. It is a creative effect with a deep and well developed story behind, and the delivery was fairly well done.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;But&nbsp;I&nbsp;have&nbsp;two&nbsp;major&nbsp;issues&nbsp;that&nbsp;lead&nbsp;ultimately&nbsp;to&nbsp;a&nbsp;downvote.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>First, this is definitely not my cup of tea about the Foundation, and I'm not sure if this kind of project belongs to the mainlist. For obvious reasons I can't be sure about my judgment on this, but I think Project EXPUNGED would be better placed on a tale.</p>
<p>Second, it is too obvious, and this is a fairly grave mistake on this article. As interesting as the story is, the document is written in such a way as to create a huge plot hole. Why is the maintenance document of a higher level than the Observation one? Why such limited timing for research, without trying for alternative ways of observation? As much as a scientist of the Foundation knows that weird stuff exists, wouldn't the reference to a virulent kind of radiation bring more attention than avoid it?</p>
<p>The phenomenon itself makes some of the truth relatively easy to guess. Even without the previous holes, an average researcher could easily conclude that the D-Class leader exists on some kind of parallel reality or time, and its continued actions are affecting the main reality. And it makes it fairly obvious, again, that there is something going on on b12. The interview just makes everything crystal clear, specially with both being neutralized as witnesses at the end, and it should at least be a higher clearance document.</p>
<p>You don't need to keep the letter in the end O5 clearance, though. Since the staff of the actual Project is level 4, the letter could stay at level 4 as a piece of research data.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Maybe you could break this story in a skip and a tale, and clean the skip. Maybe you could add some twist, like the Ethics Committee finding out and unveiling the project, and the current document being really more revealing. But for now, I'll downvote this.
							</div>

								
					
